{
  "slug": "vocales",
  "number": 1,
  "part": 1,
  "title": "French vowels",
  "tagline": "The sounds English doesn't have, letter combinations and nasal vowels.",
  "goal": "Tell apart the vowel sounds English doesn't have, read vowel combinations as a single sound and recognise nasal vowels.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "posiciones",
      "title": "Three mouth positions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "French has **more than a dozen** vowel sounds, and they are **pure**: unlike English vowels, they don't glide into another sound. Most of them come from combining three lip positions worth practising in front of a mirror:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "**Spread lips**, as in a smile: the *i* in *lit*, like the «ee» in see.",
            "**Rounded lips** pushed forward: the «oo» in food, written *ou* in French: *vous*.",
            "**Rounded lips while saying «ee»**: the French *u* of *tu*, *lune*, *rue*. It's the key sound of the language."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "lit",
              "tr": "bed",
              "note": "spread lips"
            },
            {
              "fr": "vous",
              "tr": "you",
              "note": "rounded lips"
            },
            {
              "fr": "tu",
              "tr": "you (informal)",
              "note": "rounded lips saying «ee»"
            },
            {
              "fr": "tout · tu",
              "tr": "all · you",
              "note": "compare them"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "How to find the French u",
          "text": "Say «eeee» and, without moving your tongue, round your lips as if to whistle. The sound that comes out is the *u* in *tu*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "simples",
      "title": "Single vowels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sounds",
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "a",
              "hint": "Like the «a» in father, but shorter",
              "examples": [
                "ami",
                "chat",
                "la"
              ],
              "ipa": "[a]"
            },
            {
              "label": "i / y",
              "hint": "Like «ee» in see, short and tense",
              "examples": [
                "lit",
                "vie",
                "stylo"
              ],
              "ipa": "[i]"
            },
            {
              "label": "o",
              "hint": "Like «o» in go without the «w» glide; sometimes more open, as in «lot»",
              "examples": [
                "mot",
                "vélo",
                "porte"
              ],
              "ipa": "[o] / [ɔ]"
            },
            {
              "label": "u",
              "hint": "Rounded lips saying «ee»",
              "examples": [
                "tu",
                "lune",
                "rue"
              ],
              "ipa": "[y]"
            },
            {
              "label": "é",
              "hint": "Like «ay» in say, but without the «y» glide",
              "examples": [
                "été",
                "café",
                "bébé"
              ],
              "ipa": "[e]"
            },
            {
              "label": "è / ê",
              "hint": "Like the «e» in bed",
              "examples": [
                "mère",
                "fête",
                "tête"
              ],
              "ipa": "[ɛ]"
            },
            {
              "label": "e without accent",
              "hint": "A short, relaxed «uh»; at the end of a word it's almost always silent",
              "examples": [
                "le",
                "petit",
                "table",
                "porte"
              ],
              "ipa": "[ə] or silent"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "The most common mix-up",
          "text": "The French *u* is not the «oo» in food. *tu* (you) and *tout* (all) are different words and sound different."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "combinaciones",
      "title": "Vowel combinations",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In French, several vowels in a row usually make **a single sound**. Don't read them letter by letter."
        },
        {
          "type": "sounds",
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "ou",
              "hint": "«oo» in food",
              "examples": [
                "vous",
                "rouge",
                "loup"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "oi",
              "hint": "«wa» in want",
              "examples": [
                "moi",
                "roi",
                "voiture"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "au / eau",
              "hint": "A pure «o»",
              "examples": [
                "jaune",
                "beau",
                "eau",
                "chapeau"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "ai / ei",
              "hint": "«e» in bed",
              "examples": [
                "lait",
                "maison",
                "neige"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "eu / œu",
              "hint": "Rounded lips saying «ay»; close to the vowel in «her» without the r",
              "examples": [
                "deux",
                "bleu",
                "fleur",
                "sœur"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "*eau* is three letters for a single «o». French writes far more than it pronounces."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "nasales",
      "title": "Nasal vowels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "When a vowel is followed by **n** or **m** in the same syllable, air also flows through the nose and the n or m itself is not pronounced."
        },
        {
          "type": "sounds",
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "an, am, en, em",
              "hint": "[ɑ̃]: a nasal «ah»",
              "examples": [
                "enfant",
                "chambre",
                "temps"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "in, im, ain, ein, un",
              "hint": "[ɛ̃]: a nasal «e» as in bed",
              "examples": [
                "vin",
                "pain",
                "plein",
                "simple",
                "un"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "on, om",
              "hint": "[ɔ̃]: a nasal «o»",
              "examples": [
                "bon",
                "nom",
                "maison",
                "pont"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "*un* used to have its own sound; in today's French from France it sounds almost the same as *in*."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "A quick test",
          "text": "Pinch your nose and say *bon*. If the sound changes a lot, you're making the nasal correctly. And don't add an «ng» at the end: *bon* is not «bong»."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "When there's no nasal",
          "text": "There's no nasal if the n or m is followed by **another vowel** or **another n or m**: *bon* → *bonne* (bo-nuh), *an* → *année*, *un* → *une*."
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "bon · bonne",
              "tr": "good (m · f)",
              "note": "Nasal → not nasal"
            },
            {
              "fr": "cousin · cousine",
              "tr": "cousin (m · f)"
            },
            {
              "fr": "américain · américaine",
              "tr": "American (m · f)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You'll see this again when forming the feminine [[2]]: adding the *-e* often makes the nasal disappear."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
